-- title:
--   Weil differentials bounded by a divisor are λ_{fω_0}
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, subject to the curve-theoretic hypotheses of the project: `IsCurveOver K F` (principal divisors, each place having residue field finite over $K$, and $\Omega_{F/K}$ free of rank one over $F$), `HasCanonicalDivisor` (each non-zero Kähler differential $\omega$ admits a divisor whose value at every place $v$ is $v.\mathrm{ordDifferential}\,\omega$), at least one place, at every place $v$ the condition that $v.\mathrm{dCoord}$ spans $\Omega_{F/K}$ over $F$, non-triviality of $\Omega_{F/K}$, and a choice of canonical local residue data at every place. Assume further the Riemann index formula $\mathrm{indexOfSpecialty}(D)=\ell(D)-(\deg D+1-g)$ for all divisors $D$, and the Weil–Kähler agreement: for every non-zero $\omega$ the functional `weilOfKaehler` of $\omega$ is non-zero, annihilates the sum of the adeles bounded by $\mathrm{canonicalDivisorOf}\,\omega$ and the global subspace, and $\mathrm{canonicalDivisorOf}\,\omega$ is maximal with that property. Fix $\omega_0\neq 0$ in $\Omega_{F/K}$, a divisor $D$, and a $K$-linear functional $\mu$ on the adele space annihilating the submodule spanned by the adeles bounded by $D$ together with the global subspace. Then either $\mu=0$, or $\mu=$ `weilOfKaehler` of $f\cdot\omega_0$ for some $f\in F$, $f\neq 0$. No bound on the divisor of $f\omega_0$ is asserted.
--
--   This is the statement that every Weil differential bounded by a divisor is represented by a Kähler differential, i.e. that the space of Weil differentials is the $F$-line generated by $\lambda_{\omega_0}$. It is used to transport operators defined on Kähler differentials to the spaces $\Omega_F(D)$, in the Hecke assembly for the supersingular exact sequence ([`ModularCurve.SSHeckeV2.exists_omegaHecke_dualMap_theta_and_exit`](thm.html#ModularCurve.SSHeckeV2.exists_omegaHecke_dualMap_theta_and_exit)).
